obviously the film does a wonderful job  of telling us a little bit about the  history of HIV and AIDS  but what was it what was it like to make  a film in that climate what was the  setting of the time the landscape that  you were making this important film well  we were all living it for sure I mean  this was our experience that we were  living with we had no I we had no  perspective at that point really we just  didn't know where we were in the course  of the epidemic except to know that it  was only gonna get worse but we had no  sense of when it might get better I felt  that we were blessed in a way that we  this combination here had the  connections and these whatever we had  what it took to get this paid for at a  time when people didn't do stuff on AIDS  until they did news stories but I mean  for me it was why I got into the film  business was to be able to do things  that actually could matter and I think  that's true for all of us here so I mean  it was depressing of course but it just  I felt like okay we can do something  because you felt powerless it was the  scariest time because every one of my  single friends were dying or sick and I  even thought I was sick for a minute I'm  not thank God but it was such a scary  time and we were motivated by anger and  injustice and you know Reagan not  mentioning the word AIDS it was just a  terrible terrible time and these guys  were very heroic and certainly  we also I've been doing a film like this  is in some ways it's a it's a form of  emotional protection because you feel  like you're doing something so it's a  way to focus your you know all those  feelings and emotions that you know  you're experiencing but we also knew it  was an opportunity I mean once HBO came  on board we knew we were making a film  for a mainstream audience and that  hadn't been done yet so there was a  certain responsibility that went along  with that but also a great opportunity  how long has it been since you've seen  the panels that are featured in the film  and yeah what's it like to see it today  well for me I I I just think my friends  my friends who should be here and that  makes me very sad and it also makes me  very grateful and it makes me very  grateful that I've had so much more  opportunity than they got to have in  their lifetimes and they're with us  always it made me think of of how a  community can come together and fight  back in the face of really daunting odds  and it made me just wish that people  were inspired to rise up and figure out  ways of fighting the the forces that we  need to be fighting right now  i-i've had I can't believe that I'm  saying this but I've had 2700 friends  that died of AIDS and I have a list and  if I didn't keep that list that would  remember them and my whole life since 81  has been all about grief and loss and  when I heard about this thing the 30  year anniversary and the doubt fest and  that the Academy and you know these  three gentlemen we're gonna put this on  again I said to myself this will give a  chance for young people to actually see  what we went through and it's really  powerful  [Applause]
 

